WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.394 --> 00:00:02.625
- He was an American computer programmer,

00:00:02.625 --> 00:00:06.189
entrepreneur, organizer,
and internet hack-tivist.

00:00:06.189 --> 00:00:07.735
He was involved in the development

00:00:07.735 --> 00:00:10.803
of the web feed format system RSS

00:00:10.803 --> 00:00:13.446
and the social news sharing site Reddit.

00:00:13.446 --> 00:00:15.514
He founded the group Demand Progress,

00:00:15.514 --> 00:00:18.642
which campaigned against
the stop online privacy act.

00:00:18.642 --> 00:00:20.794
He's Aaron Swartz and here are his top ten

00:00:20.794 --> 00:00:22.294
rules for success.

00:00:25.186 --> 00:00:28.849
- You know, I feel very
strongly that it's not enough

00:00:28.849 --> 00:00:30.614
to just live in the world as it is,

00:00:30.614 --> 00:00:32.433
to just kind of take what you're given

00:00:32.433 --> 00:00:36.163
and follow the things that
adults told you to do,

00:00:36.163 --> 00:00:37.562
and that your parents told you to do

00:00:37.562 --> 00:00:39.588
and that society tells you to do.

00:00:39.588 --> 00:00:40.933
I think you should always be questioning.

00:00:40.933 --> 00:00:43.312
I take this very scientific attitude;

00:00:43.312 --> 00:00:45.306
that everything you've
learned is just provisional,

00:00:45.306 --> 00:00:47.462
that it's always open to recantation,

00:00:47.462 --> 00:00:49.414
or refutation, or questioning.

00:00:49.414 --> 00:00:51.831
I think the same applies to society.

00:00:51.831 --> 00:00:55.530
I felt growing up, I
slowly had this process

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:57.300
of realizing that all the things around me

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:58.882
that people had told me were just the

00:00:58.882 --> 00:01:01.936
natural way things were, the
way things always would be,

00:01:01.936 --> 00:01:03.010
they weren't natural at all.

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:04.122
They were things that could be changed.

00:01:04.122 --> 00:01:05.504
They were things that more importantly

00:01:05.504 --> 00:01:07.495
were wrong and should change.

00:01:07.495 --> 00:01:10.144
Once I realized that, there
was really no going back.

00:01:10.144 --> 00:01:11.910
I couldn't fool myself into saying,

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:13.051
"Oh, I'm just going to go work for a

00:01:13.051 --> 00:01:14.814
business and ignore all that."

00:01:14.814 --> 00:01:17.910
Once I realized that there
were real serious problems,

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:20.783
fundamental problems that I
could do something to address,

00:01:20.783 --> 00:01:22.730
I didn't see a way to forget that,

00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:24.081
I didn't see a way not to.

00:01:24.081 --> 00:01:27.228
Do not, I repeat, do not
underestimate how much your

00:01:27.228 --> 00:01:29.854
user use will help you
to define your product.

00:01:29.854 --> 00:01:32.812
If you launch without having
significant user feedback time,

00:01:32.812 --> 00:01:34.788
you've essentially thrown away a massive

00:01:34.788 --> 00:01:37.593
and free focus group study.

00:01:37.593 --> 00:01:38.753
I think that's very important.

00:01:38.753 --> 00:01:40.727
We all kind of assume when
we're going into startups

00:01:40.727 --> 00:01:42.782
that we have this great, brilliant idea

00:01:42.782 --> 00:01:44.897
that if only we make it just
the way it is in our heads,

00:01:44.897 --> 00:01:46.866
everyone will love us and give us money.

00:01:46.866 --> 00:01:48.864
We'll be successful and famous.

00:01:48.864 --> 00:01:50.664
In reality, that's just not how it works.

00:01:50.664 --> 00:01:53.183
You hear stories all the
time of people like Flickr

00:01:53.183 --> 00:01:55.149
where the idea was completely
wrong going into it.

00:01:55.149 --> 00:01:58.024
Changed 350 degrees, spun around,

00:01:58.024 --> 00:01:59.978
tried something completely different.

00:01:59.978 --> 00:02:02.390
The more you postpone
that, the more you postpone

00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:04.840
interacting with real people
and confronting your idea

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:07.721
with the test of reality, the
more time you're just wasting

00:02:07.721 --> 00:02:10.300
working on an idea that
won't actually be successful.

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:12.848
Instead, the important thing
is to get it out there fast,

00:02:12.848 --> 00:02:14.768
confront it with real people, real users,

00:02:14.768 --> 00:02:17.145
and figure out what
makes those people happy.

00:02:17.145 --> 00:02:18.910
I think my main reaction to it was

00:02:18.910 --> 00:02:20.729
kind of being excited about it, right.

00:02:20.729 --> 00:02:22.027
Part of what made it magical was

00:02:22.027 --> 00:02:23.591
finding other people to connect with.

00:02:23.591 --> 00:02:25.051
The more and more people who were there,

00:02:25.051 --> 00:02:26.437
the more people you had to connect with,

00:02:26.437 --> 00:02:28.492
the more interested in
pages they were to visit,

00:02:28.492 --> 00:02:31.013
the more stuff that was online.

00:02:31.013 --> 00:02:32.621
You would search for stuff and the

00:02:32.621 --> 00:02:34.498
vast majority of the time,
nothing would come back

00:02:34.498 --> 00:02:36.056
because there were only
a handful of people

00:02:36.056 --> 00:02:38.034
writing stuff for the internet.

00:02:38.034 --> 00:02:40.343
As everybody started putting
things up on the internet,

00:02:40.343 --> 00:02:43.373
as every company had a
webpage, you could suddenly,

00:02:43.373 --> 00:02:45.424
instead of, I remember it
used to be so annoying.

00:02:45.424 --> 00:02:47.891
You would open up the
phone book, find the store,

00:02:47.891 --> 00:02:49.609
dial the stores number and ask them,

00:02:49.609 --> 00:02:50.990
"How late are you open?"

00:02:50.990 --> 00:02:53.042
Now you can just type
in their domain name.

00:02:53.042 --> 00:02:55.447
There would be their hours,
location, their menu.

00:02:55.447 --> 00:02:56.525
It would all be there.

00:02:56.525 --> 00:02:58.422
You wouldn't have to, at your fingertips,

00:02:58.422 --> 00:02:59.594
you wouldn't have to talk to anybody.

00:02:59.594 --> 00:03:01.634
It would just be instantly available.

00:03:01.634 --> 00:03:04.413
I remember just, I couldn't wait until

00:03:04.413 --> 00:03:05.882
the whole world was on the internet.

00:03:05.882 --> 00:03:07.878
I never had to call anybody again.

00:03:07.878 --> 00:03:10.891
Like I said, I got interested
in educational stuff.

00:03:10.891 --> 00:03:13.339
I don't think I really got
involved in a political campaign.

00:03:13.339 --> 00:03:16.313
I spent a lot of time after that wondering

00:03:16.313 --> 00:03:18.198
what it is that I could really affect.

00:03:18.198 --> 00:03:20.926
I did a lot of writing,
and a lot of reading,

00:03:20.926 --> 00:03:23.929
but a lot of the stuff I
read about social change

00:03:23.929 --> 00:03:25.762
seemed to come from this model.

00:03:25.762 --> 00:03:27.812
In the revolutions in
the 60's, people thought,

00:03:27.812 --> 00:03:31.075
"Oh, if we just get enough
people together who are angry,

00:03:31.075 --> 00:03:33.540
then all of a sudden magically,
this revolution will happen

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:36.337
and we'll take over the country."

00:03:36.337 --> 00:03:37.962
It just didn't make sense to me.

00:03:37.962 --> 00:03:41.387
I think now I have more
background, more context,

00:03:41.387 --> 00:03:43.511
I think it came out of this experience of

00:03:43.511 --> 00:03:46.391
watching the Soviet Union
because the Soviet Union

00:03:46.391 --> 00:03:49.699
was so underdeveloped, and
there weren't very many

00:03:49.699 --> 00:03:50.804
political structures in place.

00:03:50.804 --> 00:03:52.742
It was true that a small group of people

00:03:52.742 --> 00:03:54.255
getting a bunch of people
angry could kind of

00:03:54.255 --> 00:03:56.080
take over the whole country.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:57.773
I just don't think that
can happen in developed

00:03:57.773 --> 00:04:00.558
countries like the U.S. or Ireland.

00:04:00.558 --> 00:04:02.160
I began wondering, what is it that

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:04.811
you can do in developed countries.

00:04:04.811 --> 00:04:07.642
Everything seems so
ineffective and so powerless.

00:04:07.642 --> 00:04:10.100
It wasn't until just
recently that I started

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:12.126
thinking, "Okay, the
internet provides this

00:04:12.126 --> 00:04:16.152
opportunity now to get
candidates elected."

00:04:16.152 --> 00:04:18.116
It used to be there was just no way

00:04:18.116 --> 00:04:20.077
for a small group of
people to go up against

00:04:20.077 --> 00:04:22.199
the power of big money,
but one of the things

00:04:22.199 --> 00:04:25.364
we've seen in P triple C is
that there's just a couple of us

00:04:25.364 --> 00:04:28.560
who work there, and in the
past year, using nothing,

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:32.146
but basically computers
and our own apartments,

00:04:32.146 --> 00:04:34.601
we've gotten 300,000
people to join our list

00:04:34.601 --> 00:04:37.343
and raise 1.25 million dollars.

00:04:37.343 --> 00:04:39.047
That's just, three people were able to

00:04:39.047 --> 00:04:40.657
make a huge difference like that.

00:04:40.657 --> 00:04:43.123
That, I think, means the
internet really provides

00:04:43.123 --> 00:04:46.361
this chance where we can start
taking on big corporations.

00:04:46.361 --> 00:04:48.345
What made Reddit grow was people

00:04:48.345 --> 00:04:49.802
spreading it by word of mouth,

00:04:49.802 --> 00:04:51.504
saying, "This is a site I really like.

00:04:51.504 --> 00:04:55.188
I found some cool links on
it, you should check it out."

00:04:55.188 --> 00:04:58.284
What happened, the way
to get people to do that

00:04:58.284 --> 00:05:00.315
was for it to be a site
that they came back to

00:05:00.315 --> 00:05:01.978
day after day, it became
part of their routine

00:05:01.978 --> 00:05:03.758
that they felt comfortable with.

00:05:03.758 --> 00:05:06.135
Whenever we added features,
that meant changing something.

00:05:06.135 --> 00:05:07.744
Whenever you change something,

00:05:07.744 --> 00:05:09.449
your users tend to get mad at you.

00:05:09.449 --> 00:05:11.232
They liked it the way,
they were used to the way

00:05:11.232 --> 00:05:12.928
it worked before, so
they think they liked it

00:05:12.928 --> 00:05:14.415
the way it worked before.

00:05:14.415 --> 00:05:17.148
They will quibble about any
little change that you make

00:05:17.148 --> 00:05:18.690
because it's clear that
things can be changed.

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:20.367
It makes them feel
uncomfortable that this site

00:05:20.367 --> 00:05:22.261
they've gotten used to is changing.

00:05:22.261 --> 00:05:24.837
I think by having a solid
stable site each day,

00:05:24.837 --> 00:05:26.538
that made them feel
more comfortable with it

00:05:26.538 --> 00:05:28.562
and more comfortable about sharing it.

00:05:28.562 --> 00:05:31.166
It was that sharing that
got us all our traffic.

00:05:31.166 --> 00:05:32.734
The thing about the creative process is

00:05:32.734 --> 00:05:35.202
everything is built on something else.

00:05:35.202 --> 00:05:37.565
Shakespeare stories were
built on traditional

00:05:37.565 --> 00:05:39.370
legends and folktales right.

00:05:39.370 --> 00:05:40.903
Modern stuff is built on Shakespeare.

00:05:40.903 --> 00:05:42.418
Everything builds on something else.

00:05:42.418 --> 00:05:44.850
There's no completely new creative thing.

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:47.294
If only because if you wrote
something completely new,

00:05:47.294 --> 00:05:48.737
nobody would understand it.

00:05:48.737 --> 00:05:50.158
We all have to use words that were

00:05:50.158 --> 00:05:50.998
developed by someone else.

00:05:50.998 --> 00:05:53.343
We use ideas that were
developed by someone else.

00:05:53.343 --> 00:05:55.040
Everything has this
process of pulling things

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:57.072
together and re-combining them.

00:05:57.072 --> 00:06:00.152
So, what's worrying about
this sort of Copyright police

00:06:00.152 --> 00:06:02.461
is that they want to
prevent re-combination.

00:06:02.461 --> 00:06:03.801
They want to have the law come in and say,

00:06:03.801 --> 00:06:05.969
"Re-combination isn't legal."

00:06:05.969 --> 00:06:08.159
That only, you have to get a license for

00:06:08.159 --> 00:06:10.184
every single thing you use.

00:06:10.184 --> 00:06:12.426
Well, if you do that,
you stifle creativity.

00:06:12.426 --> 00:06:14.702
It's almost impossible
to get a license for

00:06:14.702 --> 00:06:15.744
every single thing you use.

00:06:15.744 --> 00:06:18.359
Imagine if every word you
use, you had to call up

00:06:18.359 --> 00:06:19.975
the person who came up with that word,

00:06:19.975 --> 00:06:21.678
or if they're dead, their descendants,

00:06:21.678 --> 00:06:23.812
and say, "Can I have
permission to use this word?"

00:06:23.812 --> 00:06:25.794
You'd never get through a sentence.

00:06:25.794 --> 00:06:29.075
Similarly, we see creativity stifled.

00:06:29.075 --> 00:06:32.028
Songs pull samples from all
sorts of different songs.

00:06:32.028 --> 00:06:33.928
Imagine if you had to call up each person

00:06:33.928 --> 00:06:36.260
you borrowed a note from, or a tune from,

00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:38.795
or a sample from and get permission.

00:06:38.795 --> 00:06:40.589
All sorts of music
would become impossible.

00:06:40.589 --> 00:06:42.500
It would become illegal.

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:44.497
I think that's what we're
seeing in the wider culture

00:06:44.497 --> 00:06:48.017
is that this notion that become
a permission asking society,

00:06:48.017 --> 00:06:50.696
that ever time you do something,
you have to ask permission.

00:06:50.696 --> 00:06:53.594
I mean, that's basically against the

00:06:53.594 --> 00:06:54.910
freedom culture we have here.

00:06:54.910 --> 00:06:56.895
That's the opposite of a free country.

00:06:56.895 --> 00:06:59.071
The idea in America is, unless it's

00:06:59.071 --> 00:07:01.512
specifically outlawed, you get to do it.

00:07:01.512 --> 00:07:04.236
The idea of Copyright laws is
people want to propose it is,

00:07:04.236 --> 00:07:06.092
unless you specifically get permission,

00:07:06.092 --> 00:07:07.482
you just can't say it.

00:07:07.482 --> 00:07:11.139
The thing that really got
me thinking along this line

00:07:11.139 --> 00:07:13.800
was right before I went to
college, I read two books.

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:16.391
I read a book, "Moral
Mazes" by Robert Jackall,

00:07:16.391 --> 00:07:18.566
which is the study of
how corporations work.

00:07:18.566 --> 00:07:20.306
It's actually a fascinating book.

00:07:20.306 --> 00:07:23.132
This sociologist, he kind of
picks a corporation at random,

00:07:23.132 --> 00:07:24.994
and just goes and studies
the middle managers.

00:07:24.994 --> 00:07:27.077
Not the people who do
any of the grunt work,

00:07:27.077 --> 00:07:29.090
and not the big decision
makers, just the people

00:07:29.090 --> 00:07:32.100
who's job is to make sure that
things day to day get done.

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:34.343
He shows how even though
they're all perfectly

00:07:34.343 --> 00:07:36.736
reasonable people, perfectly nice people,

00:07:36.736 --> 00:07:39.275
you'd be happy to meet any of them,

00:07:39.275 --> 00:07:40.643
all the things that
they were accomplishing

00:07:40.643 --> 00:07:42.562
were just incredibly evil, right.

00:07:42.562 --> 00:07:46.777
So, you have these people,
just this average corporation.

00:07:46.777 --> 00:07:48.826
They're making decisions
to blow out the workers

00:07:48.826 --> 00:07:51.001
in their factory's eardrums,
to poison the lakes

00:07:51.001 --> 00:07:53.347
and the lagoons nearby,
to make these products

00:07:53.347 --> 00:07:54.834
that were filled with toxic chemicals

00:07:54.834 --> 00:07:56.398
that poison their customers.

00:07:56.398 --> 00:07:57.765
Not because any of them were bad people

00:07:57.765 --> 00:07:59.124
that wanted to kill their workers,

00:07:59.124 --> 00:08:01.135
and their neighborhood,
and their customers,

00:08:01.135 --> 00:08:02.070
but just because that was the

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:04.779
logic of the situation they were in.

00:08:04.779 --> 00:08:06.942
The other book I read was the
book "Understanding Power"

00:08:06.942 --> 00:08:09.898
by Noam Chomsky, which kind of took this

00:08:09.898 --> 00:08:12.932
same sort of analysis and
applied it to a wider society

00:08:12.932 --> 00:08:15.465
which said, "We're in a situation where

00:08:15.465 --> 00:08:17.776
it may be filled with
perfectly good people,

00:08:17.776 --> 00:08:19.431
but they're in these
structures that cause them

00:08:19.431 --> 00:08:23.838
to continually do evil, to
invade countries, to bomb people,

00:08:23.838 --> 00:08:26.818
to take money from poor people
and give it to rich people,

00:08:26.818 --> 00:08:29.488
to do all these things that are wrong."

00:08:29.488 --> 00:08:31.537
Those books really
opened my eyes about just

00:08:31.537 --> 00:08:34.364
how bad this society we
were living in really is.

00:08:34.364 --> 00:08:35.922
There's a battle going on right now;

00:08:35.922 --> 00:08:38.682
a battle to define everything
that happens on the internet

00:08:38.682 --> 00:08:42.103
in terms of traditional things
that the law understands.

00:08:42.103 --> 00:08:44.613
Is sharing a video on
BitTorrent like shoplifting

00:08:44.613 --> 00:08:46.882
from a movie store, or is it like loaning

00:08:46.882 --> 00:08:49.234
a videotape to a friend?

00:08:49.234 --> 00:08:51.195
Is reloading a webpage over and over again

00:08:51.195 --> 00:08:53.316
like a peaceful virtual sit in,

00:08:53.316 --> 00:08:56.473
or a violent smashing of shop windows?

00:08:56.473 --> 00:08:59.950
Is the freedom to connect
like the freedom of speech,

00:08:59.950 --> 00:09:02.735
or like the freedom to murder?

00:09:02.735 --> 00:09:06.829
This bill would be a huge,
potentially permanent loss.

00:09:06.829 --> 00:09:08.235
If we lost the ability to communicate

00:09:08.235 --> 00:09:10.639
with each other over the internet,

00:09:10.639 --> 00:09:12.751
it would be a change
to the Bill of Rights.

00:09:12.751 --> 00:09:14.753
The freedoms guaranteed
under our constitution,

00:09:14.753 --> 00:09:16.993
the freedoms our country had been built on

00:09:16.993 --> 00:09:19.425
would be suddenly deleted.

00:09:19.425 --> 00:09:22.241
New technology, instead of
bringing us greater freedom

00:09:22.241 --> 00:09:23.777
would have snuffed out fundamental rights

00:09:23.777 --> 00:09:26.675
we'd always taken for granted.

00:09:26.675 --> 00:09:29.758
I realized that day, talking to Peter

00:09:30.653 --> 00:09:32.308
that I couldn't let that happen.

00:09:32.308 --> 00:09:33.141
- [Narrator] Aaron often wrote

00:09:33.141 --> 00:09:36.202
candidly in his personal blog.

00:09:36.202 --> 00:09:38.332
- [Narrator] I think deeply about things,

00:09:38.332 --> 00:09:41.227
and I want others to do likewise.

00:09:41.227 --> 00:09:44.082
I work for ideas and learn from people.

00:09:44.082 --> 00:09:45.860
I don't like excluding people.

00:09:45.860 --> 00:09:47.959
I'm a perfectionist,
but I won't let that get

00:09:47.959 --> 00:09:50.411
in the way of publication.

00:09:50.411 --> 00:09:52.626
Except for education and entertainment,

00:09:52.626 --> 00:09:54.250
I'm not going to waste my time on things

00:09:54.250 --> 00:09:55.750
that won't have an impact.

00:09:55.750 --> 00:09:57.906
I try to be friends with everyone,

00:09:57.906 --> 00:10:01.208
but I hate it when you
don't take me seriously.

00:10:01.208 --> 00:10:02.718
I don't hold grudges.

00:10:02.718 --> 00:10:06.631
It's not productive, but I
learn from my experience.

00:10:06.631 --> 00:10:10.314
I want to make the world a better place.

00:10:10.314 --> 00:10:12.353
- I think the most important
thing is to realize

00:10:12.353 --> 00:10:15.231
that you can accomplish something.

00:10:15.231 --> 00:10:17.808
I know sometimes it feels,
you just feel powerless,

00:10:17.808 --> 00:10:19.756
that you're one small person in this world

00:10:19.756 --> 00:10:21.961
of big corporations and big evil people,

00:10:21.961 --> 00:10:23.822
and big media companies, and so on.

00:10:23.822 --> 00:10:25.553
There's just nothing you can do,

00:10:25.553 --> 00:10:27.634
but the fact is, a lot of
the reason it seems like that

00:10:27.634 --> 00:10:29.650
is because people feel powerless.

00:10:29.650 --> 00:10:31.957
People are afraid to do anything.

00:10:31.957 --> 00:10:34.204
For a long time, I felt,
I watched the news,

00:10:34.204 --> 00:10:36.881
and all I saw was this
corporate propaganda,

00:10:36.881 --> 00:10:38.602
and this kind of anti-activism attacks,

00:10:38.602 --> 00:10:40.652
and I thought the news media was just

00:10:40.652 --> 00:10:42.953
inevitably biased against
us, that there was just

00:10:42.953 --> 00:10:45.008
no hope, that the only
solution was to create

00:10:45.008 --> 00:10:46.630
alternative news streams.

00:10:46.630 --> 00:10:48.855
Now, at the P-trip I found,
it's not that the news

00:10:48.855 --> 00:10:50.789
media is inevitably biased against us.

00:10:50.789 --> 00:10:52.559
It's that, you know,
reporters like all of us

00:10:52.559 --> 00:10:54.013
are just kind of lazy.

00:10:54.013 --> 00:10:56.333
They report the stories
that people give them.

00:10:56.333 --> 00:10:58.101
There are huge companies
that are willing to

00:10:58.101 --> 00:10:59.771
write up stories for them
and hand them to them

00:10:59.771 --> 00:11:03.036
on a silver platter, so all
they have to do is type them up.

00:11:03.036 --> 00:11:05.483
You know, of course
they're going to do that.

00:11:05.483 --> 00:11:06.951
It turned out, when we did the same thing

00:11:06.951 --> 00:11:09.008
when we started writing press
releases, and we started

00:11:09.008 --> 00:11:10.958
going to reporters and
pitching them stories.

00:11:10.958 --> 00:11:12.477
They were just as happy to write about us

00:11:12.477 --> 00:11:14.457
as they were about Coca Cola.

00:11:14.457 --> 00:11:17.512
So, you know, because I had
believed so long that change

00:11:17.512 --> 00:11:20.576
was impossible, it precluded
me from taking any actions

00:11:20.576 --> 00:11:22.418
that could have caused that change.

00:11:22.418 --> 00:11:24.093
So, I think the first step
for everyone out there

00:11:24.093 --> 00:11:26.801
is just to believe that you
can accomplish something.

00:11:26.801 --> 00:11:28.496
Because, once you
believe that, you're half

00:11:28.496 --> 00:11:31.179
the way to actually doing something.

00:11:31.179 --> 00:11:32.584
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:11:32.584 --> 00:11:36.189
I made this video because
Moshin Sharhan asked me to.

00:11:36.189 --> 00:11:38.124
So, if there's a famous
entrepreneur that you

00:11:38.124 --> 00:11:40.945
want me to profile next,
leave it in the comments below

00:11:40.945 --> 00:11:42.471
and I'll see what I can do.

00:11:42.471 --> 00:11:44.190
I'd also love to know which of Aaron's

00:11:44.190 --> 00:11:46.945
top ten rules had the
biggest impact on you.

00:11:46.945 --> 00:11:49.661
Leave it in the comments and
I'll join in the discussion.

00:11:49.661 --> 00:11:51.025
Thank you so much for watching.

00:11:51.025 --> 00:11:54.608
Continue to believe,
and I'll see you soon.

